1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:09,548
On Friday the thirteenth of April, 2029, a mountain of
rock will pass between the Earth and its own satellites.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:11,196
It will not hit us.

3
00:00:11,196 --> 00:00:17,991
We have known that for years. And that is exactly
what makes this night unlike any other in history.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,527
The story begins in June
2004, at Kitt Peak in Arizona.

5
00:00:27,527 --> 00:00:33,212
Three astronomers catch a faint
new asteroid, and then lose it.

6
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:39,428
That December, it is found again. And
the orbit calculations turn alarming.

7
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:48,227
For a few days, the odds of an impact on the thirteenth
of April, 2029, climb to two point seven percent.

8
00:00:48,227 --> 00:00:58,131
One chance in thirty-seven. It reaches level four on the Torino
scale, the highest rating any asteroid has ever been given.

9
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:04,066
It is later named Apophis, after
the Egyptian serpent of chaos.

10
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:09,080
But a number like that
measures what we don't know.

11
00:01:09,080 --> 00:01:20,764
Every new observation narrows the range of possible paths. Within days,
older images turn up, and the Earth slips outside that range. 2029 is safe.

12
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:24,763
Smaller risks lingered for later decades.

13
00:01:24,763 --> 00:01:34,056
Then, in March 2021, radar tracked Apophis precisely enough
to settle it. No impact, for at least a hundred years.

14
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:41,359
So what is it? A stony asteroid, about
three hundred and forty metres across.

15
00:01:41,359 --> 00:01:50,536
Radar suggests it is elongated, perhaps two lobes joined
together. It tumbles slowly, about once every thirty hours.

16
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:55,265
Here it is beside the Eiffel
Tower, at the same scale.

17
00:01:58,000 --> 00:01:59,320
Now, the distance.

18
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:11,771
This is the Earth. Out here, about thirty-six thousand kilometres up, is the ring
of geostationary satellites, the ones that carry television and weather pictures.

19
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:16,537
And far beyond them, ten
times farther, the Moon.

20
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:26,567
Apophis will arrive at about six kilometres per second, and
pass roughly thirty-two thousand kilometres above the surface.

21
00:02:26,567 --> 00:02:31,561
Inside the satellite ring. About
a tenth of the way to the Moon.

22
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:39,091
An asteroid this large is thought to come this
close only once in several thousand years.

23
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,138
And you will be able to see it.

24
00:02:43,138 --> 00:02:54,744
No telescope needed. That evening, across Europe, Africa and western Asia, around
two billion people will be able to look up into a clear, dark sky, and find it.

25
00:02:55,600 --> 00:03:02,274
A point of light, about as bright as the stars of the
Little Dipper, drifting through the constellations.

26
00:03:02,274 --> 00:03:07,542
At its fastest, it will cross the width
of the full Moon in under a minute.

27
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:13,586
It will not look like a rock. Just
a star that refuses to stay still.

28
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:18,878
For Apophis, the encounter
changes everything.

29
00:03:18,878 --> 00:03:30,067
Earth's gravity will bend its path, and widen its orbit around the Sun. Its
year will stretch from about nine tenths of ours, to nearly one point two.

30
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:38,162
The pull may also change the way it tumbles, and
could even set off small landslides on its surface.

31
00:03:38,162 --> 00:03:42,172
Nobody knows for sure.
Which is why we are going.

32
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:56,681
NASA's OSIRIS-APEX, the spacecraft that brought home pieces of asteroid Bennu, is
already on its way, due to arrive just after the flyby, if its funding survives.

33
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:09,714
Europe's Ramses, approved in late 2025 and now being built, is due to launch
in 2028 and arrive two months early, to watch the before, and the after.

34
00:04:09,714 --> 00:04:13,873
A Japanese probe is
planned to fly past, too.

35
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:26,617
And if it had been on target? To be clear, this will not happen. But a rock
this size would strike with the energy of roughly a thousand megatons of TNT:

36
00:04:26,617 --> 00:04:31,430
more than twenty times the most
powerful bomb ever detonated.

37
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:37,210
Enough to devastate a whole region.
Not enough to end civilisation.

38
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:43,224
Instead, we get something no
generation has had before.

39
00:04:43,224 --> 00:04:50,969
A large asteroid will pass this close, and we have
known for years, almost to the minute, where it will be.

40
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:58,013
Our ancestors might have seen an omen. A
wandering star, on Friday the thirteenth.

41
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:03,092
We will see a rock, on a path
we worked out long in advance.

42
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:11,343
So, on the thirteenth of April, 2029, if the
sky is clear, look up. And watch it miss.

